-- title:
--   Lemma 4.2 — forced comparisons: incomparable positions with equal revealed labels must be compared in round r
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 1$, $m \ge 2$, $r \ge 1$, and (for $r \ge 2$) let the dyadic schedule satisfy the standing assumption of Section 4 ($g \ge 2$, $b_{j-1} \ge 2b_j$). Let a deterministic $r$-round algorithm in the augmented model be well formed and correct on every input, and fix an input $\pi$. If two distinct positions $u$ and $v$ are incomparable in $P_{r-1}$ (the partial order generated by the outcomes of the cells compared in rounds $1,\dots,r-1$) and have the same label at every revealed scale $b_0,\dots,b_{r-3}$, then the route of round $r$ visits the cell $\{u,v\}$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: the standing assumption of Section 4.1 ("for n larger than a constant … g ≥ 2 and b_{j−1} ≥ 2b_j; we assume this throughout the section") → hypothesis hstand : 2 ≤ r → SectionFourStanding d r m; reason: the section states it once for all its results; for r = 1 there is no schedule
--   - encoding: "two positions u and v", the cell {u, v}; incomparable in P_{r−1} → u ≠ v; ¬ Pord π (r−1) u v ∧ ¬ Pord π (r−1) v u, Pord the transitive closure of the compared-and-ordered relation; reason: a cell has two distinct positions; P_t is the partial order generated by the relations
--   - encoding: a deterministic r-round algorithm of the augmented model, correct on every input; same label at every revealed scale → A : AugAlg d m r with A.WellFormed and A.Correct; π u / b_i = π v / b_i for every i < r − 2 (0-based labels, dyadic b_i); reason: an algorithm runs routes from the current configuration; the revealed scales are b_0, …, b_{r−3}
--   - encoding: [m] = {1,…,m}, ranks in [n], start position (1,…,1) → 0-based coordinates Fin m and ranks Fin (m^d); start = the all-zero configuration; reason: a shift of indices; no instance changes
-- source:
--   Wang (2026), The Movement Complexity of Sorting, arXiv:2611.01157 (held copy SHA-256 90e3d9df…), Lemma 4.2, p. 13

import Definitions.Def_MovementSorting_Exponent_Augmented

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace MovementSorting.Exponent

/-- Lemma 4.2 (p. 13): let a deterministic `r`-round algorithm of the augmented model be correct
on every input, and fix an input. If two positions `u ≠ v` are incomparable in `P_{r-1}` and have
the same label at every revealed scale `b_0, …, b_{r-3}`, then the route of round `r` visits the
cell `{u, v}`. (Section 4's standing assumption on the dyadic schedule is a hypothesis for
`r ≥ 2`.) -/
theorem lemma_4_2 (d m r : ℕ) (hd : 1 ≤ d) (hm : 2 ≤ m) (hr : 1 ≤ r)
    (hstand : 2 ≤ r → SectionFourStanding d r m)
    (A : AugAlg d m r) (hwf : A.WellFormed) (hcorr : A.Correct) (π : Input d m)
    (u v : Pos d m) (huv : u ≠ v)
    (hinc : ¬ A.Pord π (r - 1) u v ∧ ¬ A.Pord π (r - 1) v u)
    (hlab : ∀ i : ℕ, i < r - 2 →
      (π u : ℕ) / dyadicScale d r m i = (π v : ℕ) / dyadicScale d r m i) :
    ∃ z ∈ A.runRoute π (r - 1), s(z.1, z.2) = s(u, v) := by sorry

end MovementSorting.Exponent
